          "type": "turn",
          "question": "No chest pain at all, but I do get a bit puffed walking up the hill lately. Is that just age?",
          "answer": "**Urgency:** The urgency check returned **routine**, and no emergency pattern matched. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations, and it says to seek medical advice if symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained. A new change in breathing deserves a medical review, so please book one soon.\n\n**Get emergency help now if any of these occur:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that starts suddenly.\n- Being unable to speak in full sentences, or having to pause for breath between words.\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face or fingertips.\n- Drowsiness, confusion or unusual exhaustion while short of breath.\n- Any chest pain, pressure or tightness, or breathlessness with chest discomfort. (The chest pain check was flagged because you mentioned it only in the negative, so it did not trigger an emergency.)\n\n**Get prompt medical review if:**\n- The breathlessness is getting worse, or comes on with less effort than before.\n- It comes with sweating, nausea, or discomfort in the jaw, arm, neck, back or upper stomach. The guidance says these can be signs of a heart problem in older adults, even without chest pain.\n- You have known heart, lung or diabetes problems, since these raise the stakes of a delayed check.\n\n**Can this be put down to age?** I can't tell you that from the material I have. The sources I retrieved don't say whether breathlessness on exertion is a normal part of ageing. They do say that breathlessness on exertion, without chest pain, is a recognised way that heart disease can present in older people, and that the absence of pain is not reassuring. Age is therefore not a reason to skip a check.\n\n**What a clinician might want to rule out:** The symptom pattern involves the heart and the breathing system. The conditions the sources list as relevant include coronary artery disease, asthma, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and a lingering chest infection. Reflux can cause symptoms that feel like chest discomfort, but the sources say it should not be assumed to explain them. A clinician can sort out which of these, if any, applies.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
